  "title": "Lublin — the Largest City in Eastern Poland",
  "lead": "Lublin is the largest city in eastern Poland. Old Town, Castle, Majdanek. Slow travel through the Lublin region.",
  "body": "<h2>Why it's worth it</h2><p>Lublin is the largest city in eastern Poland and one of the oldest cities in Poland — it received city rights in 1317. It lies on the Bystrzyca river, in the Lublin voivodeship, at the crossroads of Polish, Ukrainian, and Jewish cultures. Lublin is a city that has everything: a beautiful Old Town, Renaissance townhouses, Baroque churches, a castle, cathedrals, and unique Jewish heritage (yeshiva, cemetery, synagogue). Add to that the villages and small towns of the Lublin region — Kazimierz Dolny, Zamość, Nałęczów — among the most beautiful in Poland.</p><p>Slow travel through Lublin and the Lublin region is a journey through a landscape that surprises with its diversity. The region has a city, villages, small towns, castles, churches, synagogues, and fields — all within a few dozen kilometres. It is worth spending 3–4 days here to truly feel this landscape. The best way to explore Lublin is on foot, and the region by car — public transport from Lublin is good, but between small towns it can be weak.</p><h2>What to see</h2><p>Lublin and the Lublin region are divided into three main parts: Lublin (centre), Kazimierz Dolny and surroundings (west), and Zamość and Roztocze (south). Exploring the region takes 3–4 days.</p><h3>Lublin</h3><ul><li><strong>Old Town</strong> — Renaissance townhouses, Kraków Gate, Market Square. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Lublin Castle</strong> — a castle with the Holy Trinity Chapel, one of the most valuable monuments in Poland. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Majdanek</strong> — a former concentration camp, now a museum. Worth it for history.</li></ul><h3>Kazimierz Dolny and surroundings</h3><ul><li><strong>Kazimierz Dolny</strong> — a small town on the Vistula, with a market square, townhouses, and castle hill. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Nałęczów</strong> — a spa with a spa park, one of the oldest in Poland. Worth it for health.</li><li><strong>Puławy</strong> — a town with the Czartoryski palace and park. Worth it for architecture.</li></ul><h3>Zamość and Roztocze</h3><ul><li><strong>Zamość</strong> — a Renaissance town, UNESCO-listed. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Roztocze</strong> — a hilly region with loess ravines. Worth it for the landscape.</li><li><strong>Zwierzyniec</strong> — a small town with a Baroque church and a former brewery. Worth it for architecture.</li></ul><h2>When to go</h2><ul><li><strong>May and June</strong> — the best time for slow travel. Warm, green, few tourists.</li><li><strong>July–August</strong> — festival season (Carnaval Sztukmistrzów, Jagiellonian Fair). Worth it for events, but crowded.</li><li><strong>September–October</strong> — second best. Autumn colours, fewer people.</li><li><strong>November–March</strong> — the city is sleepy in winter, but museums and galleries are open. Worth it for those seeking silence.</li></ul><h2>Getting there</h2><ul><li><strong>By car:</strong> ~2 hours from Warsaw (S17), ~3 hours from Kraków (DK19), ~2 hours from Rzeszów (S19).</li><li><strong>By train:</strong> Lublin has a railway station, with connections from most Polish cities (Warsaw, Kraków, Wrocław).</li><li><strong>By bus:</strong> PKS and private carriers serve most towns and small towns.</li></ul><p>Lublin is best explored on foot — the city is small, and attractions are close together. The Lublin region is best explored by car — attractions are scattered over a large area.</p><h2>How much time you need</h2><p><strong>A weekend (2 days)</strong> is the minimum — Lublin, Kazimierz Dolny.</p><p><strong>Three days</strong> is realistic for a first visit. Lublin, Kazimierz Dolny, Nałęczów, Zamość.</p><p><strong>A week</strong> lets you explore the whole region — from Lublin to Roztocze, with numerous stops.</p><h2>What to eat and where to sleep</h2><ul><li><strong>Cebularz lubelski</strong> — an onion flatbread, regional speciality.</li><li><strong>Pierogi with buckwheat</strong> — a regional version of pierogi, available at inns.</li><li><strong>Lublin żurek</strong> — rye sour soup with white sausage.</li><li><strong>Beer from Lublin breweries</strong> — several craft breweries in the region.</li></ul><p>Accommodation: Lublin (city, more options), Kazimierz Dolny (small town, guesthouses), Zamość (town, more options), Nałęczów (spa, guesthouses).</p><h2>Practical tips</h2><ul><li><strong>Guided tours</strong> — Lublin's history is complex, take a guide in the Old Town and Castle.</li><li><strong>Festivals</strong> — Carnaval Sztukmistrzów (July) and Jagiellonian Fair (August) are the biggest events in the region. Book accommodation in advance.</li><li><strong>Shopping</strong> — buy cebularz, honey, cheese, and cold cuts directly from local producers.</li><li><strong>Opening hours</strong> — some attractions (museums, synagogue) open only in season.</li><li><strong>Weather</strong> — weather can be changeable, even in July bring a jacket.</li><li><strong>Signal</strong> — good throughout the region, including in small towns.</li></ul>",
  "tip": "Climb the Lublin Castle tower at dusk — the best time to see the city panorama in warm light.",
